+<div class="page">
+  <h1>Cover letter — Clover Health, Principal Technologist (Office of the CTO)</h1>
+  <div class="gradient"></div>
+  <div class="meta-line"><strong>Terry J. Mork</strong></div>
+<div class="meta-line">+1-909-973-9479 · tmork.dev@gmail.com · <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/terryjmork/">https://www.linkedin.com/in/terryjmork/</a></div>
+<div class="meta-line"><strong>Date:</strong> April 7, 2026</div>
+<div class="meta-line"><strong>To:</strong> Clover Health — Office of the CTO / Talent (Principal Technologist requisition)</div>
+<div class="meta-line"><strong>Subject:</strong> Application — Principal Technologist, Office of the CTO</div>
+  <div style="height:10px"></div>
+  <p>Dear Hiring Manager,</p>
+<p>I am applying for the <strong>Principal Technologist, Office of the CTO</strong> role because it describes the kind of work I already gravitate toward: walk in, understand what is broken, scope something <strong>small enough to ship</strong>, and deliver a solution that moves the business—then improve or replace it when the long-term architecture is ready. Your posting’s line about building the <strong>bicycle, not the race car</strong> matches how I have operated in large, regulated-feeling customer environments (retail and omnichannel), where “perfect in six months” is often the enemy of “working next week.”</p>
+<p>In recent roles I have <strong>shipped</strong> while staying <strong>hands-on</strong>. At Bath &amp; Body Works I <strong>architected and delivered</strong> the end-to-end <strong>email migration</strong> from SFCC through SFMC APIs, owned <strong>authentication and deliverability</strong> work (DKIM, SPF, DMARC), and <strong>led</strong> a <strong>company-wide</strong> <strong>headless migration</strong> (SiteGenesis → PWA Kit) with explicit <strong>API contracts</strong> for auth, loyalty, and orders across <strong>US and Canada</strong>. I also <strong>directed</strong> the nationwide <strong>BOPIS</strong> rollout across <strong>114</strong> stores—exactly the sort of cross-functional, operations-heavy program where success is measured by whether stores and customers can rely on it, not by slide count. Earlier, as a <strong>Business Systems Analyst</strong> at Columbia Sportswear, I lived in the space between <strong>Product, pricing, promotions, and engineering</strong>, aligning stakeholders and third-party integrations so initiatives actually landed. I am comfortable being the person people <strong>come to first</strong> when they need a pragmatic path, and I know how to <strong>pull in</strong> the right engineers, vendors, or partners when the answer is bigger than one person.</p>
+<p>I am not a career project manager hiding behind technical vocabulary—I build <strong>integrations, automations, and workflows</strong>, and I use <strong>AI and LLM tools</strong> daily to speed research, draft specs, and automate repetitive engineering tasks, always with human review where it matters. I also know how to be the <strong>counterweight</strong> to scope creep: interim solutions should be <strong>easy to replace</strong>, bounded in dependencies, and honest about what “good enough for now” means so it does not silently become permanent debt.</p>
+<p>Clover’s mission—<strong>Medicare done differently</strong>—is a compelling reason to bring that pattern into healthcare. I will be direct: I have not spent a career inside <strong>HIPAA</strong>-labeled systems, but I have spent years on <strong>high-stakes customer data paths</strong>, production incidents, and cross-org delivery where trust and rigor matter. I learn domains quickly when paired with strong engineering and clinical partners, and I would welcome the chance to do that at Clover.</p>
+<p>Thank you for considering my application. A tailored CV for this role is available with my materials; I would welcome a conversation with the OCTO team about how I could help <strong>free leadership’s focus</strong> by owning tactical problems end-to-end.</p>
+<p>Sincerely,</p>
+<p><strong>Terry J. Mork</strong></p>
+</div>
+</body>
